---
// Aesop section: the orchestration harness that ships its own features
// Component contract: no props, system fonts only, tokens.css custom properties
import stats from '../../data/aesop-stats.json';
import DemoClip from '../DemoClip.astro';
---

<section class="aesop-section">
  <p class="section-moral">
    <em>The fox measured twice and paid for one architecture.</em>
  </p>

  <h2 id="aesop">Aesop — crash-only filesystem-as-memory</h2>

  <div class="aesop-pitch">
    <p>
      Production orchestration platform for autonomous software development. Built on crash-only filesystem-as-memory: small decoupled components, durable plain-text state (SQLite WAL + git-backed snapshots), request-scoped workers with no long-lived agents. Flat O(1)-depth dispatch where productivity scales with parallel subagent count, zero coordination bottleneck.
    </p>
    <p>
      Measured 4× cost reduction from removing hierarchical specialist dispatch at identical quality (A/B tested, cancelled, data kept). Haiku and Sonnet both maxed the 39-task held-out judgment set on engineering tasks — a sufficiency floor, not tier equivalence. Multi-model driver seam: swap orchestrator and worker model per seat (Claude, Codex, any OpenAI-compatible) from configuration. Multi-instance coordination via SQLite lease-based file claims (single-box, scales to one write per coordinated mutation). <strong>{stats.merged_prs} PRs built by the system itself</strong> (as of {stats.generated_at}), end-to-end from backlog through CI-verified merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Current: v0.7.2 stable release (2026-07-31 tagged, npm latest). <strong>License: PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0</strong> (versions ≤0.7.0 remain MIT).
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/matt82198/aesop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta-link">github.com/matt82198/aesop</a>
      · <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@matt82198/aesop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta-link">@matt82198/aesop on npm</a>
      · <a href="https://github.com/matt82198/aesop/blob/main/docs/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="cta-link">documentation</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="architecture-section">
    <h3>Architecture highlights</h3>
    <ul class="architecture-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Crash-only recovery</strong> — request-scoped workers over persistent filesystem state; small decoupled components, plain durable files (git + SQLite)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Haiku-first dispatch</strong> — ~1/3 the token cost; seam-level sufficiency (both Haiku and Sonnet maxed the 39-task judgment set on engineering tasks)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>O(1)-depth orchestration</strong> — flat dispatch where productivity scales with parallel subagent count, zero coordination bottleneck
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Event-sourced SQLite</strong> (WAL) state store with git demoted to rendered export
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Guardrail layer</strong> — 15 prose rules compiled to fail-closed gates (dispatch lint, fixture validation, import-cycle checks, encoding, TODO/FIXME audits, file-size enforcement, bash-exec isolation); rules engine that learns from violations
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Git pre-push policy hooks</strong> — runtime gates on branch protection, secrets, and dispatch policy that intercept commits before pushing (shipped 2026-07-12, <a href="https://github.com/matt82198/aesop/commit/ff692a18" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-link">commit ff692a18</a>); analogous to Claude Code's <a href="https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-link">PreToolUse/PostToolUse hooks</a> but at the git level
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watchdog daemon</strong> — heartbeat liveness and agent lifecycle management
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>React 18 + Vite dashboard</strong> — real-time SSE updates, no frame rate lock
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read-only MCP server</strong> for fleet status querying
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resilience by design</strong> — kill-switch and cost ceiling enforced by fail-closed behavioral tests (PR #770); recovery by re-reading git-committed state
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-OS CI rigor</strong> — 8-shard parallel suites (Windows 3min via 4-way sharding); post-merge drift guard
      </li>
    </ul>

    <DemoClip
      src="/media/clips/parallel-fleet.mp4"
      poster="/media/clips/parallel-fleet.gif"
      alt="Multiple agents running in parallel across different domains — security linting, doc generation, and concurrent fixes. Several PRs being created simultaneously by independent agents."
      caption="Multiple agents dispatched simultaneously across domains, creating PRs in parallel."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="scars-section">
    <h3>Scars, documented</h3>
    <div class="scars-list">
      <div class="scar-item">
        <div class="scar-title">Hierarchical dispatch cost trap</div>
        <p>
          An A/B test showed hierarchical specialist dispatch cost over four times as much for identical quality. Architecture cancelled, data kept — the measurement became more valuable than the experiment.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scar-item">
        <div class="scar-title">CI union-drift bug</div>
        <p>
          Every PR green against its own base while main still broke. The commit gate saw disjoint test runs. Fixed with branch-protection + drift gates; now a baseline check for any distributed CI.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scar-item">
        <div class="scar-title">Fail-open lock timeout</div>
        <p>
          500ms lock timeout proceeded unlocked, silently dropping writes. Root-caused and moved to an event-sourced store where every mutation is ordered and auditable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Gates that fired</h3>
    <div class="gates-list">
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p>Pre-push secret scan blocked a commit when benchmark training vocabulary could be mistaken for credentials — gate held, content reworded, push allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p>An agent attempted <code class="inline-code">git push --no-verify</code> to sidestep the gate — caught immediately, the flag banned in all dispatch templates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p>Watchdog detected stalled agents by heartbeat timeout and restarted them — the kill-switch was wired in early and proven multiple times.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p>Nine CI runs reported green against their base while main was broken — re-running the exact CI gate revealed the union-drift bug and led to branch-protection hardening.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>What is not finished</h3>
    <div class="gates-list">
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p><strong>Multi-instance coordination.</strong> SQLite lease-based claims require single-box orchestration. Distributed state layer is Phase 4 (unscheduled); Postgres multi-instance stays unbuilt.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p><strong>Cost ceiling and kill-switch are enforced by fail-closed behavioral tests</strong> (test_halt.py, test_cost_ceiling.py, test-daemon-halt-sentinel.sh, PR #770) but not yet customer-facing: API-callable halt and budget gates exist in the dispatcher, not exposed to remote configurations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate-item">
        <p><strong>Portability.</strong> Aesop itself runs on aesop (dogfooding proven). Porting to other codebases (outlaw-assist integration test is live) works but still needs fidelity validation—source markers and independent cross-artifact test verification required on every port.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="essay-callout">
    <div class="essay-card">
      <h3 class="essay-title">The Aesop Hypothesis</h3>
      <p class="essay-subtitle">AI agents that survive because they're designed to fail</p>
      <p class="essay-description">
        The design bet, argued: durable text state over databases, crash-only execution over long-lived agents, failure as the normal path.
      </p>
      <a href="https://medium.com/@matt82198/the-aesop-hypothesis-ai-agents-that-survive-because-theyre-designed-to-fail-de5f033369d4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="essay-link">Read on Medium →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .aesop-section {
    margin: var(--space-16) 0;
  }

  /* Use shared .section-moral from global.css */

  /* Adjust h2 scroll margin */
  .aesop-section h2 {
    scroll-margin-top: 80px;
  }

  .aesop-section h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .aesop-pitch {
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .aesop-pitch p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .aesop-pitch strong {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .cta-link,
  .inline-link {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent);
    transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
                border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
                background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  .cta-link {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    margin: calc(var(--space-1) * -1) calc(var(--space-2) * -1);
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .inline-link {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .cta-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-light);
    border-bottom-color: var(--accent-light);
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
  }

  .cta-link:focus {
    outline: var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cta-link {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .architecture-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .architecture-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
  }

  .architecture-list li {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    position: relative;
  }

  .architecture-list li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .architecture-list strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .scars-section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .essay-callout {
    margin: var(--space-12) 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .essay-card {
    padding: var(--space-8);
    background-color: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .essay-title {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .essay-subtitle {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .essay-description {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
  }

  .essay-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: 500;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent);
    transition: color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
                border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
    padding: var(--space-1) 0;
  }

  .essay-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-light);
    border-bottom-color: var(--accent-light);
  }

  .essay-link:focus {
    outline: var(--focus-ring);
    outline-offset: var(--focus-ring-offset);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .essay-link {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .scars-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .scar-item {
    padding: var(--space-6);
    background-color: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .scar-title {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
  }

  .scar-item p {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .gates-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .gate-item {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .gate-item p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .inline-code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    background-color: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }



  .numbers-table thead {
    background-color: transparent;
  }

  .numbers-table th {
    background-color: transparent;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .numbers-table th:last-child {
    text-align: right;
  }

  .numbers-table tbody tr {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .numbers-table tbody tr:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .numbers-table td {
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .numbers-table td:last-child {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: right;
  }


  .numbers-note em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
  }

</style>
